@GMFinX @JamieFInvests Nothing quite like putting your net worth in your display name and then campaigning for reform because one of your liabilities keeps accruing interest

@GMFinX @JamieFInvests Why does it need reforming?

Ok, your phone contract is irrelevant. RPI +3% wasn’t sprung on you, that was the formula from the start. You can argue the formula itself is too punitive, but that’s a different argument. 

More importantly, your £128 monthly repayment isn’t a function of the £51k balance or the interest rate. It’s a function of your income… so if the argument for reform is simply that RPI +3% is too high, then make that argument. Comparing it to an £18 phone contract makes no sense. 

So at some point this gets fairly simple… you borrowed money to fund university (which is a privilege in itself). Why should the principle of having to repay money you chose to borrow be considered unfair?
